24 I. INTRODUCTION
25 Biological membranes are dynamic and deformable sur26 faces whose shape and function are critically influenced by 27 the presence of protein inclusions (PIs) [1,2]. Such PIs often 28 impose geometric constraints, e.g., contact angles between 29 the protein and the membrane, or membrane displacement, 30 that drive local membrane deformations [3] and may gov31 ern membrane-mediated interactions [4]. Understanding how 32 these deformations arise and interact is central to elucidate key 33 biological processes, such as cell-shape regulation and protein 34 sorting [5]. 35 Membrane deformations induced by PIs have been the 36 subject of numerous investigations [6]. From the modeling 37 standpoint, they may be described by the shape equa38 tion [7–9], whose variational formulation stems from the free 39 energy originally proposed by Helfrich [10]. Studies on such 40 membrane deformations in the literature focus on the small 41 deformation (SD) regime [11–14], and rely on perturbative 42 expansions about the flat-membrane configurations. 43 However, a wide variety of physical situations may fall 44 beyond the SD scenario. For instance, a PI moving at large 45 enough velocities may induce a strong membrane invagina46 tion. Also, multiple PIs positioned close enough to each other, 47 may induce strong membrane deformations and gradients 48 which cannot be described as SD.
*Contact author: michele.castellana@curie.fr
In this work, we explore the interaction between a lipid 49 membrane and a PI, in the large-deformation (LD) regime, 50 leveraging the finite element (FE) method to solve the problem 51 numerically. We then focus on the features resulting from this 52 solution, such as the membrane shape, flows, the membrane- 53 mediated interaction between two PIs, and others, and discuss 54 their physical interpretation. 55 The paper is structured as follows. In Sec. II we discuss the 56 state of the art on membrane-mediated forces. Section III con- 57 tains our results in the LD regime. In particular, in Sec. III A 58 we focus on the steady state in the absence of flows. After 59 discussing the limitations of the SD solution in Sec. III A 1, 60 in Sec. III A 2 we present an approximate, analytical solution 61 for the LD regime. In Sec. III A 3 we discuss the force exerted 62 by the membrane on the PI, and in Sec. III A 4 the membrane- 63 mediated interaction between a pair of PIs. In Sec. III B we 64 study the steady state in the presence of flows by focusing 65 on the effect of inflow velocity on membrane shape. Finally, 66 Sec. IV is devoted to the discussion and interpretation of the 67 results. 68
II. STATE OF THE ART 69
