76 Other studies have addressed the LD regime [8,18], analyzing 77 both membrane shape and interaction forces; however, they 78 model proteins as pointlike inclusions, thereby neglecting 79 finite-size effects and geometric constraints imposed by the 80 proteins, such as the contact angle at their boundaries. 81 On top of numerous experimental studies—see, for ex82 ample, Refs. [3,4,19]—membrane-mediated forces between 83 proteins have been subject of interest and research in the past 84 few decades from the theoretical standpoint. However, the 85 existing theoretical studies are restricted to SD—see below. In 86 the pioneering study of Goulian et al., a power-law interaction 87 between protein inclusions has been found in the SD limit 88 [11], and later on studied for conical inclusions, in the SD 89 regime, as a function of lateral membrane tension [12] and 90 of the inclusion contact angle [13]. Studies with more than 91 two inclusions showed, for SD, the presence of nonpairwise 92 forces between the inclusions, which allow for the formation 93 of membrane-bound protein aggregates [14]. In addition, the 94 power-law dependence on inclusion symmetries was studied 95 in the SD limit [20]. The nature of this interaction was then 96 studied, in the SD regime, as function of the “hardness” of 97 the protein-protein interaction potential [21]. A geometrical 98 approach has been recently proposed and used to derive force99 distance relations in the SD regime [22]. Expressions for the 100 interparticle forces, not restricted to SD, have been derived by 101 leveraging the system symmetries [23], but they lack quanti102 tative predictive power in the LD regime due to the absence of 103 a full numerical solution. 104 Regarding the effect of membrane flows on membrane 105 shape, Arroyo and DeSimone [24] showed that membrane vis106 cosity plays a crucial role in the relaxation dynamics of fluid 107 membranes, highlighting the importance of viscous effects 108 in dynamical models. In Ref. [25,26], membrane instabilities 109 have been studied in simple geometries, showing that vis110 cous effects can be characterized by a dimensionless number 111 governing the influence of membrane viscosity on shape. In 112 Ref. [27] the effect of nonzero membrane curvature on the 113 diffusion coefficient of PIs has been evaluated analytically, 114 extending a result previously obtained in Ref. [28], where the 115 effect of membrane deformation due to PIs in static condi116 tion had been studied. In both cases, the curvature-generation 117 mechanism has been considered as separated from membrane 118 flow. In Ref. [29], the effect of membrane tension on the 119 diffusion coefficient of PIs that locally deform the membrane 120 by imposing a finite angle is analyzed analytically within a 121 perturbative framework, valid in the limit of small imposed 122 angles. Finally, in Ref. [30], coupled mean-field equations de123 scribing the time evolution of protein density and membrane 124 shape were derived, accounting for the interplay between cur125 vature elasticity and transport processes. 126 Regarding previous numerical studies, several works 127 [31–35] have proposed FE methods to model fluid 128 membranes—see Ref. [36] for a detailed overview on the 129 features and limitations of such FE studies.
