388 IV. DISCUSSION
389 We have studied, by combining analytical and numerical 390 methods, the deformations in a cell membrane induced by 391 PIs in the LD regime. Our numerical analysis allows for go392 ing beyond current perturbative approaches [11–14,20,21,23], 393 which cannot describe the LD regime. 394 We first focused on the steady state of a lipid membrane 395 with a single PI with axisymmetry, where the membrane is 396 pinned on an outer circle with radius R and the PI is located at 397 the center. We showed the limitations of perturbative solutions 398 by comparing them to numerically exact, FE solutions [36], 399 for large protein displacements or large PI contact angles; 400 see Fig. 2. We developed an analytical solution for the shape 401 equations in the LD regime, which assumes that the mean 402 membrane curvature vanishes. Although this solution is only 403 approximate, it yields a sensible, analytical expression of the 404 membrane shape, see Fig. 4. According to this solution, a PI 405 with a fixed contact angle has a spontaneous displacement that 406 scales, for large R, as ∼ ln R; see Eq. (15). Moreover, for a 407 straight contact angle α = π /2, the spontaneous displacement 408 remains finite; see Eq. (15). We show that the vertical compo409 nent of the force exerted by the membrane on the PI—the only 410 nonzero component due to rotational symmetry—displays a 411 nonmonotonic dependence on the vertical displacement h of 412 the PI. This behavior is analogous to the one obtained when 413 a pointlike force is applied to a membrane [8], demonstrating 414 that the detailed size of the PI does not affect qualitatively the 415 force profile. 416 We then analyzed the force exerted by the membrane on 417 the PI, in the LD regime. The force displays a nonmonotonic 418 dependence on the vertical displacement of the PI, reminiscent
of that of a pointlike force applied to a membrane [8]. We 419 assesses how this nonmonotonic behavior is affected by the PI 420 contact angle, as well as by the size of the membrane domain; 421 see Fig. 5. 422 In addition, we numerically studied the membrane- 423 mediated interaction between two PIs in the LD regime. As 424 shown in Fig. 8(A), for the values of the interprotein dis- 425 tance d that we considered, the potential does not display 426 a power-law behavior. Instead, the potential shape shows a 427 sub-power-law decay, i.e., it decreases with d slower than a 428 power law. This nontrivial structure is due to the complex 429 medium which conveys the interaction—the lipid membrane 430 [55]. Finally, Fig. 8(b) shows that the potential is an odd 431 function of the PI orientation, and that two PIs with the same 432 orientation yield a larger energy than two PIs with opposite 433 orientations. This behavior confirms the interpretation [12] 434 that the angle imposed by each protein can be thought of as 435 a “charge,” i.e., PIs with the same and opposite orientations 436 repel and attract each other, respectively. 437 In the presence of membrane flows, we identified the emer- 438 gence of a characteristic velocity v∗, of the order of a few 439 microns per second, which reflects the competition between 440 bending and viscous forces. At low velocities, membrane 441 flows have negligible impact on membrane shape. However, at 442 higher velocities, flow-induced deformations appear. We pro- 443 vided a theoretical estimate for the critical velocity at which 444 this deformation arises, which explains their characteristic 445 length and aligns well with numerical results. The presence 446 of this characteristic velocity scale suggests that PIs motion 447 may significantly influence long-range membrane deforma- 448 tions and thus, for instance, membrane-mediated interactions. 449 An example of how flow-induced membrane deformations 450 can influence interactions between PIs is given by protein 451 clustering on giant unilamellar vesicles (GUVs). In this re- 452 gard, we consider an example involving bacteriorhodopsin 453 (BR)—the best understood ion-transport protein [56]. Multi- 454 ple BR molecules have been incorporated and diffuse [57] on 455 the membrane of a GUV [58]. A large GUV with a ∼100 μm 456 diameter populated with ∼104 BRs, yields an average inter- 457 protein distance d ∼ 2 μm. Given that the BR lateral diffusion 458 coefficient of ∼1.2 μm2/s, the typical values of BR lateral 459 diffusion velocity is v ∼ 2 μm/s. According to the analysis of 460 Sec. III B, this implies a flow-induced membrane deformation 461 with wavelength λ ∼ κ/(ηv) ∼ 1.5 μm, comparable with the 462 interprotein spacing. Given that this wavelength is compa- 463 rable to the spacing between BRs, flow-induced membrane 464 deformations may imply a potential, significant effect on BR 465 interactions and patterning on the GUV surface. 466 Future work could leverage our results to study PI clus- 467 tering in membranes. In fact, our analysis may be extended 468 to study systems with more than two PIs, examining how 469 collective effects influence membrane deformation and forces, 470 both with and without membrane flows. This analysis can also 471 provide novel insights on PI clusters [55,59], as well as on the 472 effect of membrane-mediated forces on the cluster assembly 473 mechanisms and scaling laws. Finally, a full stability analysis 474 of the governing equations may reveal the existence of flow- 475 driven instabilities at larger velocities, with implications for 476 membrane trafficking [2] and mechanosensitive processes in 477 cells [60]. 478
